---
import RootLayout from '../layouts/RootLayout.astro';
import { metricLabels, overallWeights } from '../data/config';
---

<RootLayout title="How Astra scores and recommends AI models | Methodology"
  ><div class="page-heading">
    <span class="eyebrow">NOTHING BEHIND THE CURTAIN</span><h1>
      Understand the score.<br />Then make your own call.
    </h1><p>
      Numbers should make a decision clearer. Here’s what ours mean, where they
      come from, and where they stop.
    </p>
  </div>
  <article class="prose">
    <section id="sample-data">
      <h2>First, a note about this edition.</h2><p>
        This local edition contains 12 fictional models from fictional
        providers. Every score, price, capability, release date, and model
        description is an illustrative fixture created on September 5, 2026.
        None is a claim about an available AI product. The catalog is
        deliberately excluded from search indexing.
      </p><p>
        The fixture source is <code>src/data/models.ts</code>. Synthetic inputs
        demonstrate the calculation pipeline; they are not public benchmarks or
        internal evaluation runs. Evidence confidence is 0% because these
        fixtures have no real supporting measurements.
      </p><p>
        Recommendations and cost estimates let you test the complete experience.
        Do not use them to make a real purchasing decision.
      </p>
    </section>
    <section>
      <h2>One scale, different strengths.</h2><p>
        Capability scores range from 0 to 100. Each evidence record stores the
        raw measurement, its scale, its source, and its date. Normalization
        converts it to a common range:
      </p><p><code>100 × (raw − minimum) / (maximum − minimum)</code></p><p>
        Values are clamped to 0-100 and rounded to whole numbers. In this
        sample, each capability uses one synthetic input on a 0-1,000 scale.
        Real benchmarks need documented bounds, direction, task comparability,
        and validation before they can contribute. Normalization alone does not
        make incompatible tests comparable.
      </p>
    </section>
    <section>
      <h2>Overall quality. Not just the cheapest answer.</h2><p>
        The overall score is a weighted sum of the individual capabilities,
        rounded to a whole number. Price has only a 2% influence. Specialized
        rankings keep each capability visible independently.
      </p><div class="weight-grid">
        {
          Object.entries(overallWeights).map(([key, value]) => (
            <div>
              <>
                <span>{metricLabels[key as keyof typeof overallWeights]}</span>
                <strong>{Math.round(value * 100)}%</strong>
              </>
            </div>
          ))
        }
      </div><p>
        These are provisional product weights, version <code>sample-1.0</code>.
        They live in one configuration file and are exposed on model detail
        pages. Cost efficiency is a synthetic capability in this edition, not a
        measured economic advantage.
      </p>
    </section>
    <section>
      <h2>Confidence is about evidence.</h2><p>
        Our confidence function combines independent source count (20%), source
        quality (20%), recency (15%), internal test count (15%), consistency
        (10%), and capability coverage (20%). Source count saturates at five,
        test count at 50, freshness decays over 180 days, and variance is scaled
        over 25 points.
      </p><p>
        This is an evidence-coverage heuristic, not a statistical probability
        that a score is correct. All fictional fixture scores have 0%
        confidence. No number of synthetic measurements can establish real-world
        confidence.
      </p>
    </section>
    <section>
      <h2>A recommendation you can follow.</h2><p>
        The finder filters out models that do not fit your API task budget or
        required vision support. It then combines the chosen task score (55%),
        your priority (30%), and reliability (15%). Quality uses intelligence;
        speed uses speed; cost and value use cost efficiency; balanced uses
        overall.
      </p><p>
        Ties are resolved by lower estimated task cost and then model slug. The
        best match has the highest fit score. The value choice has the highest
        cost-efficiency score among remaining eligible models. The alternative
        is the next available fit. No sponsorship changes these results.
      </p><p>
        Free means zero paid API cost. Very cheap means at most $0.005 per
        sample task, moderate means at most $0.03, and flexible applies no price
        cap. A sample task uses 1,000 input and 500 output tokens.
      </p>
    </section>
    <section>
      <h2>The cost of a completed task.</h2><p>
        Token prices tell only part of the story. Our calculator includes input
        and output usage, optional tool costs, and an assumed retry rate.
      </p><p>
        <code>Expected task cost = cost per attempt / success probability</code>
      </p><p>
        This assumes constant cost and independent retries. The default is one
        successful attempt. Workload sizes are illustrative assumptions, not
        observed usage averages. API fees are separate from subscription plans,
        taxes, hosting, and provider-specific charges.
      </p>
    </section>
    <section>
      <h2>Facts, evidence, and estimates stay separate.</h2><p>
        Provider documentation is the intended authority for price, context,
        modalities, and availability. Public evaluations and controlled internal
        testing supply evidence. Derived scores refer back to those
        measurements. Cost estimates retain their workload assumptions.
      </p><p>
        The import validator requires source references, dates, score
        methodology, evidence coverage, bounded values, and unique model slugs.
        Verified imports cannot contain mock sources or synthetic evidence.
        Imports are archived as new snapshots; existing historical snapshots are
        not overwritten.
      </p><p>
        Structural validation cannot establish whether an external claim is
        true. A human source review is required before a verified import is
        selected for publication. This edition has not performed that review or
        connected to a live provider feed.
      </p>
    </section>
    <section>
      <h2>Freshness is part of the answer.</h2><p>
        Model facts retain the source update date and last verification date;
        scores retain their calculation date; pricing retains its effective
        snapshot date. A fixture creation date never masquerades as provider
        verification. Old values remain in history rather than being silently
        replaced.
      </p><p>
        Use scores to narrow a choice, then review the evidence and test a model
        on your own work. Small score differences rarely tell the whole story.
      </p>
    </section>
  </article>
</RootLayout>
